  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 132 PM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThursday...The relatively balmy weather for the latter half of \nNovember, continues with strong southwest flow aloft inducing \nsurface lee troughing. Gusty south and southwest winds will persist \nbased on projected gradients. Still dry with the paucity of low and \nmid level moisture availability. 700 mb temperatures near 1 Celsius \nwill yield maximum temperatures from the mid 40s to mid 50s.\n\nFriday...An initial shortwave trough aloft, or atmospheric \nperturbation, will encroach on our western counties early in the \nday, spreading widespread orographic and dynamically driven rain and \nsnow showers across Carbon County Wyoming and our Snowy and Sierra \nMadre Ranges. Then, after midday, the scattered to numerous rain and \nsnow showers will spread eastward across the rest of southeast \nWyoming, with the typical downsloping limiting shower coverage \nalong and east of the Laramie Range and Interstate 25 corridor.\nA typical blustery cool day is on tap, with maximum temperatures in \nthe 40s to lower 50s.\n\nInterestingly, climatologically, November 20th, 1900, is the latest \nfirst measurable snowfall event for Cheyenne in the Autumn, with 0.1 \ninches of snow or greater. The verdict is still out as to whether \nCheyenne will officially see the 0.1 inch snowfall criteria met this \nday, due to uncertainties, as it is a coin flip whether or not a \nrogue snow shower affects Cheyenne. Unfortunately, downsloping and \nthe speed and location of the shortwave trough aloft passage and its \nassociated cold front, will limit any precipitation accumulation \neast of I-25, although our Snowy, Sierra Madre and Laramie Ranges \nwill fare better with decent accumulations possibly in at least \nWinter Weather Advisory criteria and possibly more.\n\nSaturday...As the shortwave trough aloft sweeps quickly into the \nPlains states, strong westerly flow aloft develops across our \ncounties, with our typical wind prone locations such as Arlington, \nBordeaux and the I-80 Summit possibly seeing strong winds, possibly \nexceeding High Wind criteria. Drying out from west to east during \nthe day in the wake of the shortwave trough aloft. Cheyenne may \nindeed see its first measurable snow before noon on November 21st. \nFive days before Thanksgiving, an even colder day is anticipated \nwith readings from the mid 30s to mid 40s with 700 mb temperatures \naround 6 Celsius.\n\nSunday...The progressive pattern synoptically continues, with our \nflow aloft once again backing to southwest in advance of the next \napproaching shortwave trough aloft over southern Nevada, backing our \nsurface winds to southeasterly to the east of the Interstate 25 \ncorridor. Paucity of low and mid level moisture spells dry \nconditions continuing. Not quite as cold with highs from the upper \n30s to upper 40s, based on 700 mb temperature trends and less cloud \ncover compared to Saturday.\n\nMonday...A split trough aloft moves across the Rockies, with most \nenergy and 500-300 mb and 700-500 mb quasigeostrophic forcing either \nnorth of our counties over Montana or south of our counties over New \nMexico, though scattered showers of rain and/or snow will likely \nfall in minimal amounts over our counties. Temperatures similar to \nSunday.",
